.TH PHONTEST 1
.SH NAME
phontest \- check the pronunciation lexicon say(1) reads
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B phontest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Phontest
checks
.IR phon (2)
and the lexicon it reads.
It prints
.B PASS
or a
.B FAIL
line per problem.
.SH "WHAT CAN AND CANNOT BE TESTED"
Nothing in this system can hear.
A wrong entry in the lexicon produces a word that is said wrong, and every
structural check still passes, so this test does not claim to establish that the
lexicon sounds right.
Two things can be established and both are.
.SS "The five phrases that were heard"
The narration in
.B /lib/demo/tour.sh
was originally written by hand in the voice's own symbols, and listened to,
before any of this existed.
Phonemising the English of those five phrases must give back exactly those
strings.
.PP
That pins two different things at once: the fifteen words they use, which are
the only entries in the lexicon anything has ever verified by ear, and the way
words are joined together \(em which is the part a change to
.IR phon (2)
would otherwise break silently.
Shortening one entry by a single symbol fails the phrase check and two of the
shape checks.
.SS "Every symbol is one the voice knows"
Each symbol of each entry is looked up in the model's own
.BR phoneme_id_map .
This catches the class of mistake the file is most prone to, and one instance
in particular: the voice knows the script
.B g
at U+0261 and does not know
.SM ASCII
.BR g .
In most fonts they are the same shape.
Substituting one for the other in two entries is caught by name.
.SS "The tutorials are sayable"
Every word every script in
.B /lib/demo
narrates is looked up.
The whole directory rather than a named file, so that writing a new tutorial
puts it under this check without anyone having to remember to.
Without this a tour with a missing word still fails \(em
.IR say (1)
refuses it by name \(em but it fails part way through, after some of the
narration has been spoken, which is a poor way to find out.
.PP
Also checked: a word that is not in the lexicon is refused and the error is the
word itself; with two missing, it is the first; case does not change the sounds;
punctuation the voice knows survives and spacing does not reach the sounds.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/phontest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR say (1),
.IR phon (2)
.SH BUGS
The five pinned phrases use fifteen words.
The other hundred and thirty-odd entries are checked for symbols the voice
knows and for nothing else, because there is nothing else to check them
against \(em there is no second implementation to compare with, as
.IR mltest (1)
has.
.PP
It checks
.B /lib/demo
and nowhere else.
A tutorial kept somewhere else is not covered until it is run.
